sapphire: GPU buffers are mapped write-combined by the kernel whether or not they are zeroed

With gpu_zero=0 a buffer of 256 KiB or more was never mapped by the kernel, so its pages kept no
write-combined cache mode and the program saw them as device memory (reported by HouseOfBalance/ios-sapphire,
patch 0332). The mapping is now made for every new buffer; only the bzero depends on gpu_zero. With the
default gpu_zero=1 nothing changes.
---
diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SapphireGpu.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SapphireGpu.cpp
index 0b62bba..d8f42b3 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SapphireGpu.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SapphireGpu.cpp
@@ -6627,11 +6627,18 @@ SapphireGPUClient::gemNew(struct msm_gem_new *r)
 			zero_all = z != 0;
 		}
 		bool zeroed = pm != NULL;
-		if (pm != NULL && (zero_all || size < 256 * 1024) && !(reuse && gpu_bocache == 2)) {
+		/*
+		 * 0395: the kernel maps every new buffer write-combined once, zeroed or not. A buffer's pages take their
+		 * cache mode from the first mapping; one that the kernel never mapped reached the program as device
+		 * memory, where an unaligned store traps (EXC_ARM_DA_ALIGN in the Metal driver with gpu_zero=0).
+		 */
+		if (pm != NULL && !(reuse && gpu_bocache == 2)) {
 			diag_t0 = mach_absolute_time();
 			IOMemoryMap *km = pm->map(kIOMapWriteCombineCache);
 			if (km != NULL) {
-				bzero((void *)km->getVirtualAddress(), size);
+				if (zero_all || size < 256 * 1024) {
+					bzero((void *)km->getVirtualAddress(), size);
+				}
 				km->release();
 			} else {
 				zeroed = false;
